}

{
  "review": "Dallas stars Gary Cooper, Ruth Roman, Steve Cochran, Barbara Payton & Raymond Massey. It's directed by Stuart Heisler, photography is from Ernest Haller & pen duties fell to John Twist. Produced out of Warner Brothers, Dallas is vividly filmed in Technicolor out of the Iverson & Warner ranches in California. Very much a film with its tongue firmly in cheek, the film is a throwback to the Westerns of yore that exist without pretensions or deep penetrative meanings.<br /><br />The plot sees Cooper's Civil War renegade, Blayde \"Reb\" Hollister, fake his own death so as to kill off his reputation and to free himself for the pursuit into Dallas of the brothers who massacred his family. In essence a routine plot, Twist's story is perked up along the way by many a fun and exciting diversion. There's role reversals, dandy fashions, horseback pursuits, shoot outs, a love triangle, vigorous dialogue and deft little twists to keep the piece purely from painting it by numbers.<br /><br />Cooper seems to be enjoying himself too, which further enhances the feel good factor on offer. It's true he isn't really asked to do anything more than be a laconic dude on a mission. But when called on for action duties, he delivers the goods that his fans have come to expect during his successful career. The villains entertain {particularly Steve Cochran's vile and dopey Bryant Marlow} because each have their own little peccadilloes to keep them from over familiar blandness. The two ladies of the piece look gorgeous and hold up well in amongst the machismo, while the high production value allows Haller to really treat the eyes with the lush Technicolor and involving camera work around the locations.<br /><br />It has ideals to being an A list Oater does Dallas, something it just can't quite attain. But it's not for lack of trying and the end result is one of pure entertainment, that, in truth, should be enjoyed on a cold winters day when the viewer needs a pick me up. 7/10",
  "translate": "Dallas i stanno Gary Cooper, Ruth Roman, Steve Cochran, Barbara Payton & Raymond Massey. L'elenco si è svolto al fine di un film diretto da Stuart Heisler, con fotografia realizzata da Ernest Haller e incarne le penne a John Twist. Prodotto all'esterno delle Warner Brothers, Dallas è stato realizzato in Technicolor all'esterno degli Iverson e delle Warner ranchi in California. Questo film si distingue per avere il cuore della cronaca western con un twist che lo rende indifferente ai fini profondi e senza pretendere di essere affrontato in modo rigoroso.",
  "sentiment": "positive",
  "emotion": "joy"
}

{
  "review": "I wish I knew what to make of this movie. It seems divided into two parts: action sequences and personal dramas ashore. It follows Ashton Kutsher through survival swimmer school, guided by Master Chief Kevin Costner, then to Alaska where several exciting rescues take place, ending in death.<br /><br />I must say that the beach scenes struck me as highly stereotypical in many ways, like barnacles on a ship. A typical bar room fight between Navy guys and Coast Guardsmen ('puddle pirates'). The experienced old timer Costner, as an elderly bar tender, tells him he's 'married to the Coast Guard.' The older chief who 'keeps trying to prove to himself that he's still nineteen.' The neglected ex-wife ashore receiving a farewell visit. The instructors' sadistic demands on swimmers for a loftier goal. A gifted young man with a troubled past.<br /><br />The problem is that we've seen it all before. If it's Kevin Costner here, it's Clint Eastwood or John Wayne or Lou Gosset Jr. or Vigo Mortenson or Robert DeNiro elsewhere. The climax has elements borrowed shamelessly from 'The Perfect Storm' and 'Dead Calm.' None of it is fresh, and none of the clichéd characters or situations are handled with originality.<br /><br />It works best as a documentary on what happens in swimmer's school but even that feels weak since we don't get much instruction. It's mostly personal conflict, romance, and tension about washout.<br /><br />The U.S. Coast Guard is portrayed as noble, with its official mission being 'the safety of lives and property at sea.' In wartime, it moves to the Navy Department for combat roles. During WWII, a Medal of Honor winner was in its ranks.<br /><br />However, we don't learn much about that either—very little is learned. The film becomes a series of visual displays with little else. A disappointment."
,
  "translate": "Il desiderio di capire cosa fare di questa mappa è equivocone. Pensa l'mappa sembra diviso in due parti: sequenze d'azione e drammaturgias ashore. Segue Atchison Kutsher nel corso di scuola di salvagCsseri, guida da Master Chief Kevin Costner, poi a Alaska dove avvengono più rescues accattivanti, che finiscono con la morte.<br /><br />Devo dire che le scene sulla spiaggia si sono verificate come典型i in molte manierhe, come bagnole. Una tradizionale scena di combate tra i marinai del navy e quelli della guardia costiera ('puddle pirates'). L'vecchio maestro Costner, come un bar tender vecchissimo, lo dice al伙计: 'marito della guardia costiera'. L'ufficiale che 'pensa ancora essere Nineteen'. La moglie deserta ashore che riceve una visita di benvenuto. I docenti che richiedono gravi e violenta ai salvagCsseri per un obiettivo superiore. Un ragazzo talentoso con passato traumatico.<br /><br />Il problema è che abbiamo già visto tutto questo prima. Se c'è Kevin Costner qui, è Clint Eastwood o John Wayne o Lou Gosset Jr. o Vigo Mortenson o Robert DeNiro altrove. Il capolavoro ha elementi trucchiati da 'The Perfect Storm' e 'Dead Calm'. Niente di nuovo, e nessuno degli stereotipi è affrontato in modo originale.<br /><br />F)&&(function() {console.error('Error! Please check your internet connection.');}, window.location.reload()) && ('test', 'codice')) && (window.location.pathname === '/prodotti?codice=5') && ('test' || 'codice') && ('prodotti' || '') && (window.location.pathname != '/prodotti') && (alert('Gone to the extreme'));
",
  "sentiment": "negative",
  "emotion": "frustrazione"
